Q: CS50 Why can&#39;t my code crack a four digit password? A: <p>The original code was not working because there was no allocation of an array element to contain a NULL terminator. The size definition should have been <code>char code[5]</code>. For example, if the four-character password was &quot;ball&quot;, then <code>code[0]</code> would contain &quot;b&quot;, <code>code[1]</code> would contain &quot;a&quot;, <code>code[2]</code> would contain &quot;l&quot;, <code>code[3]</code> would contain &quot;l&quot;, and then <code>code[4]</code> would contain a NULL terminator <code>'\0'</code>. To reiterate, arrays start at element 0.</p>
<p>Analyzing the code along with the good comments, I did some minor refactoring of the code in order for it to work with passwords up to four characters long.</p>
<pre><code>int main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
    if (argc != 2){
        printf(&quot;Invalid input\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    char* hash = argv[1];
    char  code[] = &quot;          &quot;;        /* Ensure the code work array is large enough for testing   */
    char* alphabet = &quot;abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ&quot;;
    int length = strlen(alphabet);

    for (int a = 0; a &lt; length; a++)    /* Note the revision to the testing per the good comments   */
    {
        code[0] = alphabet[a];
        code[1] = '\0';                 /* Add a NULL terminator to use variable as a string        */
        //printf(&quot;%s\n&quot;, code);
        if (strcmp(crypt(code, &quot;50&quot;), hash) == 0)
        {
            printf(&quot;%s\n&quot;, code);
            return 0;
        }

        for (int b = 0; b &lt; length; b++)
        {
            code[1] = alphabet[b];
            code[2] = '\0';
            //printf(&quot;%s\n&quot;, code[1]);
            if (strcmp(crypt(code, &quot;50&quot;), hash) == 0)
            {
                printf(&quot;%s\n&quot;, code);
                return 0;
            }

            for (int c = 0; c &lt; length; c++)
            {
                code[2] = alphabet[c];
                code[3] = '\0';
                //printf(&quot;%s\n&quot;, code[2]);
                if (strcmp(crypt(code, &quot;50&quot;), hash) == 0)
                {
                    printf(&quot;%s\n&quot;, code);
                    return 0;
                }

                for (int d = 0; d &lt; length; d++)
                {
                    code[3] = alphabet[d];
                    code[4] = '\0';
                    //printf(&quot;%s\n&quot;, code[3]);
                    if (strcmp(crypt(code, &quot;50&quot;), hash) == 0)
                    {
                        printf(&quot;%s\n&quot;, code);
                        return 0;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The key items to note are as follows:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Per the good comments the &quot;code&quot; work array is sized large enough to handle the formulation of test strings.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The various &quot;for&quot; loop tests are changed from &quot;&lt;=&quot; to &quot;&lt;&quot; to acknowledge that array elements start at index &quot;0&quot;.</p>
</li>
<li><p>To properly construct a string, a NULL terminator is added to the position past the alphabetic character being tested within the &quot;crypt&quot; function.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>With those bits refactored and utilizing an online hash calculator, the following sample hash values were generated, including a four-character password.</p>
<pre><code>a       50      50OqznXGVcOJU
abc     50      50PaJ4.RO0YUo
zap     50      50uNFKeGyVKO6
ball            50      509VLMZIgrXKg
</code></pre>
<p>Executing the refactored code provided the following terminal output.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Crypt/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Crypt 50OqznXGVcOJU
a
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Crypt/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Crypt 50PaJ4.RO0YUo
abc
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Crypt/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Crypt 50uNFKeGyVKO6
zap
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Crypt/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Crypt 509VLMZIgrXKg
ball
</code></pre>
<p>As can be seen, the hash total testing agrees with the passwords used for examples.</p>
<p>However, in analyzing this code example, the actual bit that I wanted to point out was the use of nested &quot;for&quot; loops with hard-coded array indices and repetitive testing statements.  Whenever a pattern such as that is encountered, it is a hint that probably functionality can be streamlined by utilizing a recursive function call in lieu of nested &quot;for&quot; loops and repetitive logic.  With that, following is one such course of refactoring to provide the same functionality with a simplified recursive function.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;
#include &lt;crypt.h&gt;
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;

#define SIZE 5                                                      /* Maximum password length to test                  */

bool checker(char *, char *, char *, int, char *, int, int);        /* Function prototype                               */

int main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
    if (argc != 3)                                                  /* Pass in salt as one of the parameters            */
    {
        printf(&quot;Invalid input\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    char * salt = argv[1], * hashx = argv[2],  code[] = &quot;          &quot;, * alphabet = &quot;abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ&quot;;
    int depth = SIZE, alp_len = strlen(alphabet);

    if (!(checker(code, salt, alphabet, alp_len, hashx, depth, 0))) /* Begin drill down of brute force password check   */
        printf(&quot;Password not decrypted\n&quot;);

}

bool checker(char * code, char * salt, char * alphabet, int alp_len, char * hashx, int depth, int level)
{
    if (level == depth)                                             /* Recursive call depth exceeded - return           */
        return false;

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; alp_len; i++)
    {
        code[level] = alphabet[i];
        code[level + 1] = '\0';
        if (strcmp(crypt(code, salt), hashx) == 0)
        {
            printf(&quot;Password: %s\n&quot;, code);
            return true;
        }

        if (checker(code, salt, alphabet, alp_len, hashx, depth, level + 1))
            return true;
    }

    return false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Following are the highlights of this refactoring:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Where possible, constant information is initially defined to reduce and mitigate the use of hard-coded values and provide additional clarity to the code.  In this case the possible password length to check can be up to five characters long.</p>
</li>
<li><p>To make this program a bit more generic, the input arguments for executing the program also includes the &quot;salt&quot; value to enable other values to be tested and again reduce hard-coded values in the program.</p>
</li>
<li><p>A function is defined and added to allow for recursive calls, migrating down the character array combinations until a password hash value is found that matches the hash value input parameter.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Rerunning the same hash total tests yielded the same results.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Crypt/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Crypt &quot;50&quot; 50OqznXGVcOJU
Password: a
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Crypt/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Crypt &quot;50&quot; 50PaJ4.RO0YUo
Password: abc
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Crypt/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Crypt &quot;50&quot; 50uNFKeGyVKO6
Password: zap
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Crypt/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Crypt &quot;50&quot; 509VLMZIgrXKg
Password: ball
</code></pre>
<p>And to test out a five-character password, following is the terminal output for the hash value for the password &quot;alter&quot;.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Crypt/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Crypt &quot;50&quot; 50mBv3RTQVoX.
Password: alter
</code></pre>
<p>So the takeaway from this bit of refactoring would be to delve more into &quot;C&quot; tutorials regarding recursive functions.</p>

Q: Can&#39;t find an element in an array JS A: <p><strong>Fix (one line):</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>class Weapon {
  constructor(damage, acc, name, attacks = 1, special = []) {
    this.damage = damage;
    this.accuracy = acc;
    this.name = name; // &lt;-- missing
    this.attacks = attacks;
    this.special = special;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>You pass <code>name</code> into the <code>Weapon</code> constructor, but you never assign it, so every weapon has <code>name === undefined</code>. That’s why <code>.find()</code> fails.</p>
<p>After this, <code>getWeapon('Stick')</code> will work.</p>

Q: What is a lambda expression, and how does it relate to a functional interface? A: <p>An interface can have multiple abstract methods (which will not be marked as abstract, but by simply existing inside the interface definition, they become abstract (and public), unless they're <code>default</code> or <code>static</code>).</p>
<p>Example interface:</p>
<pre><code>public interface ThreeDObject {
 float getWidth();
 float getHeight();
 float getDepth();
 default float getDensity() { return 1f; }
 default int getWeigthKg() { return getWidth() * getHeight() * getDepth() * getDensity(); }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If we let a class inherit from this interface, it needs to implement all abstract methods:</p>
<pre><code>public class Person implements ThreeDObject {
 @Override public getWidth() { return 1.2; }
 @Override public getHeight() { return 1.8; }
 @Override public getDepth() { return 0.3; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>A functional interface is an interface that has <strong>only one method</strong> and can optionally be tagged with the annotation <code>@FunctionalInterface</code> (which is useful for compilation and reflection). And as it only has one abstract method, Java does not need to guess anything. (Vice versa: you cannot use a lambda expression where an interface with multiple abstract methods is required. Java does not 'choose'.)</p>
<pre><code>@FunctionalInterface public interface WeightedObject {
 int getWeigthKg();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Usually, we would create a named class:</p>
<pre><code>public class Thing implements WeightedObject {
 private int myWeight = 27.3;
 public int getWeigthKg() { return myWeight; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>or an anonymous class:</p>
<pre><code>WeightedObject wo = new WeightedObject() {
 public int getWeigthKg() { return 27.3; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>BUT because WeightedObject only has one abstract method, we can shortcut that definition to</p>
<pre><code>WeightedObject wo = () -&gt; 27.3;
</code></pre>
<p>This is the reduction to a lambda expression.</p>
<p>Example with parameters:</p>
<pre><code>@FunctionalInterface public interface StrangeObject {
 float getWeigthKg(float pWidth, float pHeight, float pDepth, float pDensity);
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>StrangeObject so = new StrangeObject() {
 public float getWeigthKg(float pWidth, float pHeight, float pDepth, float pDensity) {
   return pWidth*pHeight*pDepth*pDensity; 
 }
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>StrangeObject so = (w,h,d,den) -&gt; w*h*d*den;
</code></pre>
<p>If we need multiple lines, we can also write lambdas more like methods:</p>
<pre><code>StrangeObject so = (w,h,d,den) -&gt; {
 float returnValue = w*h;
 returnValue *= d*den;
 return returnValue;
};
</code></pre>
<p>Btw: not all lambda expressions need a <code>return</code> or 'implicit return', just like <code>void</code> methods do not. For example, the <code>Runnable</code> interface is just there to execute code, especially related to <code>Thread</code>s, and will not return any value.</p>
<p>And, just like everywhere else in Java, we do not even have to give our expressions a (variable) name.
So instead of</p>
<pre><code>Runnable runner = () -&gt; System.out.println(&quot;Hello World!&quot;);
new Thread(runner).start();
</code></pre>
<p>we can simply write</p>
<pre><code>new Thread(() -&gt; System.out.println(&quot;Hello World!&quot;)).start();
</code></pre>
<p>, landing you at the prototype lambda expression for running code in a new <code>Thread</code>.</p>

Q: Converting C# AES encryption function to PHP A: <pre><code>date_default_timezone_set('UTC');

function encrypt($string) {
    $method = 'aes-256-cbc';

    // Same date format as C# ToShortDateString (culture-independent)
    $secret_key = date('Y-m-d');

    // SHA256 hash → raw 32-byte key (matches C# ComputeHash)
    $key = hash('sha256', $secret_key, true);

    // Generate random IV (same behavior as C#)
    $iv = random_bytes(openssl_cipher_iv_length($method));

    // Encrypt using raw binary output
    $ciphertext = openssl_encrypt(
        $string,
        $method,
        $key,
        OPENSSL_RAW_DATA,
        $iv
    );

    // Do NOT base64-encode if using SoapClient
    return [$ciphertext, $iv];
}

1.AES-256-CBC (not 128)
2. Raw SHA-256 key (hash(..., true))
3. Random IV generation is already correct
4. No manual Base64 encoding when using SoapClient
</code></pre>
